EUP 1.0

EUP makes installers. You write a text file that names your files and says where they go; EUP makes an installer from it. Every installer made with EUP looks and works the same way: install types in a pop-up menu, a list for Custom Install, the disk space needed, Install Location with Choose..., and a progress window with Cancel. An installer can also show a screen about your software and a license to agree to.

On this disk

EUP: the packager.
Installer Shell: the installer that EUP copies. Keep it in the same folder as EUP.
Example: an INI file and the files it installs.
Licenses: the terms of the code in EUP and in the installers it makes.

Making an installer

1. Make a folder. Put your INI file in it, and everything the installer is to install.
2. Open EUP, and open the INI file. EUP checks it and the files it names, and lists any problems by line.
3. Save the installer. EUP compresses your files into it.

Try it with the Example folder first.

The INI file

An INI file is a text file in sections. Each section starts with its name in brackets; under it, each line is a key, an equals sign, and a value. A line that starts with a semicolon is a comment.

[installer]
product = Hello
version = 1.0
description_screen = About Hello
license_screen = License

[type easy]
label = Easy Install
description = Hello and its samples.

[type custom]
label = Custom Install
custom = yes

[component hello]
label = Hello
source = Hello
flags = bundle
types = easy, custom

[component samples]
label = Samples
source = Samples
destination = %chosen%:Samples
types = easy, custom

Paths are written with colons, from the INI file's folder: Files:Samples. In destination, %chosen% is the folder the user picks; everything is installed inside it. A folder is installed with everything in it.

[installer]
product: the software's name. Needed.
version: its version, such as 1.0 or 2.0.1.
title: the window's title. Normally "Install" with the product and version.
file: the installer's name. Normally the title.
folder: the name of the folder offered for the install. Normally the product.
get_info: the version line in Get Info.
creator, icon: the installer's own creator and a file with its icon (ICN# 128).
description_screen, license_screen: text files shown before the install. The license has Agree and Disagree.

[type ID]: an install type, in the pop-up menu.
label: its name. Needed.
description, or description_file: what's shown in the box.
custom = yes: the box is a list to pick from.
remove_old = no: old names (see [upgrade]) are left alone.

[group ID]: a heading in the list.
label: the heading. Needed.
selectable = yes: the heading has a checkbox for its whole group.

[component ID]: a file or folder to install.
label, source, types: its name, the file or folder, and the types that install it. Needed.
destination: where it goes. Normally %chosen%.
group, description, checked: its group, its line in the list, and whether it's checked at first.
as: its name when installed.
type, creator, flags: the file's type and creator, and Finder flags (bundle, invisible, or a number).
text = yes: plain files are text.
compress = no: stored as is.

[upgrade]
detect: a file that marks an earlier install; the installer installs into its folder.
remove: an old name to remove. One to a line, as many as needed.
keep: a file not to replace if it's there.

[layout]
window: the installed folder's window: left, top, width, height.
place: an icon's place in it: Hello: 18, 9

Each line of a text file is a line on the screen: write a paragraph as one line, and leave a blank line between paragraphs. Texts are up to 32,000 characters.

Requirements

EUP and the installers it makes need a Mac Plus or later and System 6 or later. EUP needs 512K of memory.

